# The comment-drift state machine, locally and without a model.
#
# It is docs/working/comment-drift.tony's first half — head-moved, gather, review — with one
# thing replaced: the review spawns a BROKERED task instead of an llm one, so the drive script
# is the worker. `verse task do complete <id> '<answer>'` supplies the findings, which means the
# answer is a fixture and the TIMING is the script's: a review lands exactly when the test says
# it does.
#
# That is the whole point. The two defects this exists to reproduce are about ORDER, not about
# review quality:
#
#   - a review that finishes AFTER the head moved writes `reviewed: done` over the pending state
#     the new head left, and that commit is never reviewed;
#   - a push of several commits only ever reviews the tip, so a fix in any other commit of it is
#     never seen and its finding stays open forever.
#
# Neither is reachable in under ten minutes against staging, and both are reachable in seconds
# here. The rules below are otherwise VERBATIM from the real charter, so a fix proved here is a
# fix that transfers — when they diverge, this file is the one that is wrong.
at: verse-dev.drift
reads: [ !verse-path [git] ]
---
- name: reflected
  level: 0
  match:
    system: git

- name: drift
  level: 10
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, drift, head]

- name: ledger
  level: 20
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, drift, finding]

# A stand-in for the merge half's `train`, so the READ the real charter needs can be proved
# here: a train is above the head it describes, which makes reading the report a read DOWNWARD.
- name: train
  level: 30
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, drift, train]
---
# 1. Each tip main has had, as a fact this charter owns.
#
# A RECORD PER COMMIT, which is the change under test. `key: $subject.value.sha` makes the
# derive's address a function of (rule, subject, KEY) rather than (rule, subject), so every sha
# gets its own record — and with it its own `reviewed` state, its own subject for rule 3 to
# match, and therefore its own task id.
#
# One slot was the cause of both defects the harness holds still. A late `onDone` wrote
# `reviewed: done` over the state a newer commit had just left, and a push of two overwrote the
# first commit's record before anything could review it: neither has anywhere to land now,
# because neither commit's record is the other's.
#
# The deletes go with it: a fresh record starts empty, so there is no previous review to clear.
- name: head-moved
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject: git:head:verse
  action:
    derive:
    - path: [verse-dev, drift, head]
      id: $subject.value.sha
      value:
        repo: $subject.value.repo
        sha: $subject.value.sha
        reviewed: pending
---
# 2. Before the review, the ledger is read onto the subject.
- name: gather
  stratum: drift
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, head, "*"]
      value:
        reviewed: pending
  action:
    patch:
      reviewed: gathered
      outstanding: !list-paths
        at: [verse-dev, drift, finding, "*"]
        value:
          status: open
---
# 3. …and the tip is reviewed, once, against what is already known.
#
# The spawn is brokered rather than llm: no runner, no environment, no prompt worth reading —
# the drive script claims the task and completes it with the findings the test wants. Everything
# after the spawn (the hooks, the derive, the latch) is the real charter's.
- name: comment-drift
  stratum: drift
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, head, "*"]
      value:
        reviewed: gathered
  action:
    spawn:
      vector: auto
      prompt: |
        Review $sha. The drive script answers this task; nothing here reads the prompt.
    onDone:
      derive:
      - path: [verse-dev, drift, finding]
        value: !each
          over: $data.findings
          key: $item.symbol
          value:
            symbol: $item.symbol
            status: $item.status
            says: $item.says
            at: $item.at
      patch:
        err: !delete null
        result: $data
        reviewed: done
        reviewedSha: $data.at
    onFailed:
      patch:
        err: $err
        result: !delete null
        reviewed: failed
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        err: !delete null
        result: !delete null
        reviewed: cancelled
---
# 4. A train, carrying the report of the commit it is about.
#
# THE READ THE REAL CHARTER NEEDS, proved here. With a head record per sha there is no fixed
# address for "the report" any more — the id is (rule, subject, sha), composed by the engine so
# that a derive stays inert, and no charter can spell one. So the record is named by CONTENT: a
# predicate on the field the reader already has.
#
#     !get-path {at: [verse-dev, drift, head, "*"], value: {sha: …}, path: result.report}
#
# `!get-path` and not `!list-paths`, and that is the point of the wrinkle this rule worked out.
# The pair is split by cardinality, and a predicate USED to be the list form's alone — so the
# only way to select by content answered with a list, of one, which every reader downstream then
# had to unwrap: `subjectSays` decodes `says` as a string and renders a list of one as nothing.
# A get takes the predicate now and holds itself to the one, refusing at fire time if two match.
# What arrives here is a node.
#
# The predicate carries `reviewed: done` as well as the sha, and that is not decoration: a
# record exists from the moment its commit lands, and the report appears only when the review
# does. Naming the state in the predicate is the difference between "the report is not there
# yet" and "there is no such commit", and it is what makes the wait legible in the charter.
#
# The train is cut by the DRIVE SCRIPT rather than by a rule, which is the one place this file
# models the real charter loosely and does it deliberately. There, a train is cut on the tip and
# loaded later, when a proposal appears; the gap between the two is where the report lands. Here
# the script cuts it, so the harness says exactly when — including, in one case, before the
# review has landed at all.
- name: train-says
  stratum: train
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, drift, train, "*"]
      value: !not.has-path says
  action:
    patch:
      # ONE ENTITY, NAMED. `id: $subject.value.sha` on rule 1 (rather than `key:`) makes the
      # record's address the place plus the sha — `verse-dev.drift.head.aaaa1111` — so a reader
      # SPELLS it. With `key:` the engine composes (rule, subject, key) into
      # `head-moved@git:head:verse@aaaa1111`, which no charter can write down, and every reader
      # then has to describe the record instead: a `"*"` and a predicate on a field it happens
      # to know, with the cardinality checked at fire time. That read worked and it read badly,
      # and the badness was the address, not the operator.
      says: !get-path
        at: [verse-dev, drift, head, $subject.value.sha]
        path: result.report
